  "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\u22ef,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi =-1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi =1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =-1+\\frac{1}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{4!}(x-\\pi )^{4}+\u22ef \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}(x-\\pi )^{4}+\u22ef\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\pi +1$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi +1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi +1) & \u2248-1+\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi +1-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}(\\pi +1-\\pi )^{4} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{24} \\\\  & =-0.542,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $3$ decimal places.",
